  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nThrough Monday: Heat and humidity will be the main concern through \nthe period but local radars were showing isolated showers and \nthunderstorms developing over the eastern half of the CWA again this \nafternoon. This activity will increase over the next several hours \nwith the greatest coverage expected over the northeast. This \nactivity will see a distinct diurnal trend and dissipate shortly \nafter sunset. Latest satellite imagery/RAP analysis still showed our \nCWA in the weakness between a mid level ridge to our west and to our \nsoutheast. This weakness combined with daytime heating of our moist \nairmass will lead to at least isolated convection again Monday \nafternoon. Wl maintain a moist airmass and with temperatures \nclimbing back into the 90s and combining with high humidity, peak \nheat index values will top out between 106F and 110F for the area \nalong and just east of the Mississippi river. Elsewhere across the \nCWA less oppressive conditions are expected with peak heat index \nvalues near 105F. A Heat Advisory will remain in effect for the area \nalong and just east of the Mississippi river. /22/  \n\nThe Bermuda high will propagate westward broadly over the region \nleading to steadily decreasing rain chances and increasing \ntemperatures. Dangerous heat continues to be the primary concern \nover the next week and an \"elevated\" continues to be messaged for \nmainly west of I-55. It appears heat will peak on Wednesday as this \nis when both temperatures and dewpoint will be maximized. \nThereafter later in the week, temperatures will increase as the \nhigh strengthens and the 850 thermal ridge peaks, but \nsimultaneously this will lead to slightly lower dewpoints and \ndeeper mixing potential and thus slightly lower heat indices. The \nsummer regime is likely to persist through the period and hazard \ngraphics may be adjusted throughout. /SAS/",
